WEBVTT

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.515
Hey guys, it's Jeff Meacham.
大家好，我是杰夫·米查姆。

00:00:03.515 --> 00:00:06.750
I played the wedding DJ in the season three finale.
我在第三季季终集里演了婚礼DJ。

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.145
I believe it was 2007.
我记得那是2007年。

00:00:08.145 --> 00:00:10.175
I had just moved to Los Angeles.
我当时刚搬到洛杉矶。

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:13.093
It was the second job I ever booked out in LA.
那是我在洛杉矶接到的第二份工作。

00:00:13.093 --> 00:00:15.820
First comedy, I had booked first multi-cam.
第一部喜剧，我接的第一个多机位拍摄。

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:21.972
And as soon as I got onto set, I was like, oh, this is what I want to do for the rest of my life.
我一到片场就觉得，哦，这就是我余生想做的事。

00:00:21.972 --> 00:00:22.923
And here's why.
原因如下。

00:00:22.923 --> 00:00:23.938
We get onto set.
我们来到片场。

00:00:23.938 --> 00:00:25.334
It's this wedding set.
这是一个婚礼的场景。

00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:27.109
There's a lot of background.
有很多背景演员。

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:29.266
There's all sorts of moving parts.
有各种各样的活动部件。

00:00:29.266 --> 00:00:31.739
And I'm up in the kind of a bird's eye.
我在那种类似俯瞰的角度。

00:00:31.739 --> 00:00:44.420
to you in the DJ booth and boom, first team comes in and you guys are all carrying your coffees and your sides and your cracking jokes and having the best time together and Pam comes in and kind of orchestrates the whole scene.
看着DJ台那边的你，轰的一声，第一组演员进来了，你们都拿着咖啡和台词纸，开着玩笑，玩得非常开心，然后帕姆进来了，可以说是导演了整个场景。

00:00:44.420 --> 00:01:03.899
Neil's character comes and stands next to me and then you guys were hers and someone just yells the word suck a team and you guys just kind of funnel off together, leave, grab your coffees and you're giggling as you're leaving an income to this group of people that kind of look like you, wearing chalkboards with your character's names on them.
尼尔的角色走过来站在我旁边，然后你们就退场了，有人喊了声“切”之类的话，你们就一起嬉笑着离开，去拿咖啡，边走边咯咯笑，接着一波长得跟你们差不多的人走了进来，身上挂着写有你们角色名字的小黑板。

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:05.379
And I was like, what is this?
我就想，这是什么？

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.140
And somebody comes over and they're like, oh yeah, yeah.
然后有人走过来对我说，哦，对，对。

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.500
They basically come in rehearsing and then they leave and they get to go have the best time of their lives.
他们基本上先进来彩排，然后离开，去享受他们生命中最快乐的时光。

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.140
Well, these people come in and do all the blocking and get everything set with the lights.
好吧，这些人进来完成所有的走位，把灯光都调好。

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.180
And then they come back in and tell jokes and be stars.
然后他们再进来讲笑话、当大明星。

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.700
And I was like, oh, that's what I want to do for the rest of my life.
我就想，哦，这正是我余生想做的事。

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.459
So that really was a big turning point in my life where I was like, oh, this is amazing.
所以那真的是我人生中的一个巨大转折点，当时我就觉得，哦，这太棒了。

00:01:26.459 --> 00:01:27.299
I remember that.
我还记得。

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:28.379
I remember how kind Pam was.
我记得帕姆人有多好。

00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:38.620
In fact, I just got to work with her again recently, and how cool it was as this young 20-something where Neil comes over and stands next to me.
事实上，我最近刚和她再次合作过，作为20多岁的年轻人，当尼尔走过来站在我旁边时，那种感觉真的很酷。

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.939
I was like, hey, man, I grew up watching you.
我就说，嘿，哥们儿，我是看着你的戏长大的。

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:40.780
You're amazing.
你太棒了。

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:41.620
And he's like, thanks.
他说，谢谢。

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:49.540
I was like, oh, also, do you remember on Law & Order a couple of years ago where you played that kind of psycho killer guy where you lobotomized women by pouring hot water into their brains?
我说，哦，还有，你还记得几年前在《法律与秩序》里吗，你演了一个那种变态杀手，通过把热水灌进女人脑子里来给她们做脑叶切除手术？

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.260
And he was like, yeah, I was like, my wife was that woman.
他说，记得，我说，我老婆就是那个女人。

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.219
And he was like, cool, I was like, cool.
然后他说，酷，我说，酷。

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:55.340
So there you go.
所以就是这样。

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.700
incredible show to be on.
能参演这部不可思议的剧真是太棒了。

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.620
You guys were the best and I can't thank you enough for that experience.
你们是最棒的，我非常感谢你们带给我的那段经历。

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.559
Well, thank you, Jeff.
谢谢你，杰夫。

00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:27.580
What a great note to start the show with and what a voice he has.
以这么棒的一段话来开始节目，而且他的声音真好听。

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.369
I hope he has his own podcast, right?
我希望他有自己的播客，对吧？

00:02:29.369 --> 00:02:30.577
Let's get a little voice.
我们听听这个声音。

00:02:30.577 --> 00:02:32.511
Alec has done podcasts with Jeff, right?
亚历克和杰夫一起做过播客，对吧？

00:02:32.511 --> 00:02:33.188
Their buddies.
他们是好哥们儿。

00:02:33.188 --> 00:02:33.688
Oh, Jeff.
哦，杰夫。

00:02:33.688 --> 00:02:34.188
Yes, Jeff.
对，杰夫。

00:02:34.188 --> 00:02:36.461
And I have a podcast that's meeting Tom Cruise.
我和我有档播客叫“遇见汤姆·克鲁斯”。

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:37.460
if you can go listen to that too.
如果你有兴趣也可以去听听。

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.379
Yeah, that's awesome, that's a great story, Jeff.
太棒了，这真是一个美好的故事，杰夫。

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:50.099
I'm so glad that I didn't know that little bit you did, being in that one episode, this episode, something borrowed, meant so much to you and it was such an early part of your journey.
我很高兴我不知道你当时做的那些小事情——参演那一集，这一集《借来的东西》——对你意义如此重大，而且那是你演艺生涯中如此早期的经历。

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:54.180
That's one of the many things, you don't know, you don't know what it means in the moment.
这是许多事情之一，你当时并不知道它意味着什么。

00:02:54.180 --> 00:03:09.259
It's good to, when you're starting out in a business as crazy and unpredictable, as the one we're in, it's really good to have good early formative experiences, where you see functional working set, where people are happy, it's fun to tell stories, It's fun to be there, so we're glad we were able to provide that.
当你刚开始涉足我们这个疯狂且不可预测的行业时，拥有好的早期成型体验真的很棒，在那里你能看到一个运转正常的片场，人们都很开心，讲故事很有趣，待在那里也很开心，所以我们很高兴能提供这样的体验。

00:03:09.259 --> 00:03:10.259
They're not all like that.
并不是所有片场都这样。

00:03:10.259 --> 00:03:13.180
Not every set is like that, and yeah, that's cool.
并非每个片场都是如此，是的，这挺酷的。

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.180
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:28.340
Well, we're thrilled you could join us, and we're thrilled you who are listening to this could join us here today for another episode of How We Made Your Mother, where Craig Thomas and I talk about the TV show we made together or How I Met Your Mother.
很高兴你们能加入我们，也很高兴收听节目的各位今天能和我们一起收看又一期的《我们是如何创造你母亲的》，我和克雷格·托马斯在这里畅谈我们共同创作的电视剧《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.259
I played Ted Mosby on that show from 2005 to 2014.
我在剧中饰演泰德·莫斯比，从2005年一直演到2014年。

00:03:32.259 --> 00:03:37.479
Craig and his buddy Carter Bayes co-created the show.
克雷格和他的死党卡特·贝斯共同创作了这部剧。

00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:43.219
We did 208 episodes of the show, and we are barreling through watching them all again.
我们一共拍了208集，现在正快马加鞭地重温所有集数。

00:03:43.219 --> 00:03:45.199
And we're coming to the end of season two.
我们快要看到第二季的尾声了。

00:03:45.199 --> 00:03:46.199
How is it possible?
怎么可能呢？

00:03:46.199 --> 00:03:47.199
It's incredible.
真是不可思议。

00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:59.280
Today we're talking about episode 21, something borrowed written by Greg Malins, one of our faves who was on the show a couple episodes ago this season.
今天我们要讨论的是第21集《借来的东西》，由格雷格·马林斯编剧，他是我们最喜欢的编剧之一，本季前几集他就来过。

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.159
Now, Alec, tell us historically root us.
那么，艾利克，带我们回顾一下历史背景吧。

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:04.560
When was this, when did this episode air?
这是什么时候播出的？

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.560
Yeah, cast your mind back to May 7th, 2007.
嗯，把大家的思绪拉回到2007年5月7日。

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.039
Wow.
哇。

00:04:10.039 --> 00:04:12.360
It's a TV wedding, so it happened in May sweeps.
这是一场电视剧里的婚礼，所以它发生在5月的收视率大战期间。

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.439
We could have guessed May.
我们本来也能猜到是5月。

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:19.360
May was, May would have been the top most, that would have been number one on the Family Feud guesses.
5月绝对是……在《家庭问答》的竞猜里这绝对能排第一。

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.120
Yeah, that's, that's crazy, man.
是啊，太疯狂了，老兄。

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.240
This is a good one.
这一集很棒。

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.079
This is a good one.
这一集真不错。

00:04:24.079 --> 00:04:26.920
This begins, this is kind of part one of a two-parter.
这可以说是上下两集中的上集。

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:37.160
And Josh actually said earlier today, like it's kind of part two of a three-parter because it feels like the episode showdown that proceeds this has a few little nuggets planted in it about the wedding.
乔希今天早些时候还说，这其实像是三部曲中的第二部，因为在此之前的那集《决斗》里，似乎就埋下了几个关于这场婚礼的小伏笔。

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:42.720
You actually get a little glimpse of this wedding, Marshall's in a hat in the wedding very mysteriously.
你确实能隐约看到这场婚礼，马修在婚礼上戴着一顶帽子，非常神秘。

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.839
Marshall's in a hat, which is great.
马修戴着帽子，这太棒了。

00:04:44.839 --> 00:04:47.399
It led Jordana to say, why is Marshall wearing a hat?
这让乔丹娜忍不住问：马修为什么要戴帽子？

00:04:47.399 --> 00:04:49.800
In that weird hat, you know, wait for it?
戴着那顶奇怪的帽子，你知道的，“拭目以待”吗？

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:56.920
Yeah, and also isn't showdown where Ted and Robin come in and their spaghetti stained clothes?
对，而且《决斗》不就是泰德和罗宾穿着沾满意大利面酱的衣服登场那一集吗？

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:58.000
Yes, yes.
对，没错。

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.160
They come in and they're covered in spaghetti sauce and they say, we'll tell you that later.
他们走进来，身上全是意大利面酱，然后说：“我们回头再告诉你。”

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:09.279
And they mean it, they mean much later because you don't find out until the episode after this, but that's why these three feel like a three part finale in a way of the season.
他们是认真的，真的是“回头”——因为你要到下一集才会知道原因，但正因如此，从某种程度上讲，这三集感觉像是本季的三部分大结局。

00:05:09.279 --> 00:05:11.759
Well again, it's mysteries.
嗯，再说一次，这就是悬念。

00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:13.920
It's little mysteries woven throughout.
是贯穿其中的一个个小悬念。

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:22.000
But it also shows to me that you guys were really mapping this thing out and you were really, you wanted everything to count.
但这也向我表明，你们当时真的是在精心规划这一切，你们希望每一个细节都有意义。

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.240
You know, sometimes there's dangling threads or people comment and say, why did this happen?
你知道的，有时会有未解的悬念，或者观众会评论说，为什么会这样？因为在某某集，也就是第七季时发生了这个，而你不可能把所有事都照顾周全，但我认为你们在收尾方面做得太出色了，而且……

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:35.160
because in episode, you know, season seven, this happened, and you couldn't account for everything, but I think you guys did an amazing job tying things up and...
谢谢。

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.000
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.560
Yeah, we took it seriously.
对，我们是很认真的。

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:44.720
I was very excited because my wife hadn't seen this one a long time and I said, do you, well, I guess it was in showdown, I said this, I said, do you remember why they're covered in pasta sauce?
我当时非常兴奋，因为我妻子很久没看这一集了，我问她——哦，我想是在《决斗》那一集里我问的——我说：“你还记得他们为什么会沾满意大利面酱吗？”

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.759
And she's like, no, it didn't register for her at all.
她说：“不记得了”，她完全没注意到。

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:52.040
And she was like, she didn't think it was weird, it just kind of goes by.
她觉得这并不奇怪，剧情就这么顺下去了。

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:52.879
Yeah.
是啊。

00:05:52.879 --> 00:06:03.199
And then like when we watched, we watched the spoilers, We watched the one we're talking about after this, which is something blue, the finale of season two, and it paid off, and she was very excited, because she was like, that's the pasta sauce.
然后当我们看下一集——也就是我们要讨论的下一集，第二季大结局《某种蓝色》时，谜底揭晓了，她非常兴奋，因为她喊道：“原来那是意大利面酱！”

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:05.480
Okay, now I got it, she completely forgotten.
“好吧，我明白了，”她早就忘得精光了。

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:09.160
I forgot about it, and I had actual pasta sauce spilled onto me.
我都忘了，而且意大利面酱是真的洒在我身上。

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:10.160
And I forgot about it.
连我都给忘了。

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.639
You're the person who should have had the strongest visceral memory.
你应该是那个记忆最深刻、感触最深的人才对。

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:15.600
This vivid, vivid memory, yeah.
这种极其生动的记忆，对吧。

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:23.079
Well, yeah, I mean, I think that that speaks to, like, Carter is a crossword puzzle guy, like me.
嗯，对，我的意思是，这说明了卡特和我一样，是个填字游戏迷。

00:06:23.079 --> 00:06:28.120
But I think you both have a sense of like, puzzlemanship, is that a word like?
但我觉得你们两个都有那种“解谜感”——这是个词吗？

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:28.959
Puzzlemanship.
解谜感（Puzzlemanship）。

00:06:28.959 --> 00:06:30.480
You heard it here first, ladies and gentlemen.
女士们先生们，你们在这里第一次听到这个词。

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.339
You like things kind of falling into place.
你喜欢那种事情逐渐水落石出的感觉。

00:06:33.339 --> 00:06:36.040
You like, you got like a good mislead.
你喜欢那种，你喜欢一个很好的误导。

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:37.920
You like, what is going on?
你喜欢那种，到底是怎么回事？

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.399
Why freeze frame?
为什么要定格画面？

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:40.439
Let's back up.
让我们倒回前面。

00:06:40.439 --> 00:06:41.839
Let's show how we got there.
让我们看看我们是怎么走到这一步的。

00:06:41.839 --> 00:06:42.680
Yeah.
对。

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.120
We love telling stories like that.
我们很喜欢讲这样的故事。

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:50.160
We looked at these seasons, kind of like novels, kind of like you could jump around and really everything should refer to something else.
我们把这些季看作是一部部小说，你可以跳跃着看，而且真的每一处都应该指代另一处。

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.480
Everything should be there for a reason if it possibly can be.
如果可能的话，每一处存在都应该有它的理由。

00:06:52.480 --> 00:07:09.120
And we really looked at a season as one big organism rather than this series of standalone things, which like we said at the beginning of season two, the network would have probably been happier at the time if we had done standalone episodes that were very digestible, that required little thought.
我们确实把一整季看作是一个巨大的有机体，而不是一系列独立的东西。正如我们在第二季开始时所说的，如果我们当时拍的是那种非常易懂、不需要动太多脑筋的独立单集，电视网当时可能会更高兴。

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.279
You don't have to have seen them in any particular order.
你不需要按任何特定的顺序看它们。

00:07:11.279 --> 00:07:12.800
It's just not how we write.
但这根本不是我们的写作方式。

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.240
They asked us to do more of that.
他们要求我们多拍一点那样的。

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.959
And we kind of said, we'll try knowing that we were not going to try and that we can't.
而我们当时说，我们会试试的，但我们心里清楚我们根本不会去试，而且我们也做不到。

00:07:18.959 --> 00:07:20.959
We could only write it this way.
我们只能这样写。

00:07:20.959 --> 00:07:28.360
And I have to say rewatching it feels very vindicating for that decision because it's very fun to watch like this again, right?
我不得不说，重温它让我觉得当初的决定非常正确，因为像这样再次观看真的很解压，对吧？

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.560
It's very fun to go back and go, this is why everything was here.
回过头来看，然后说“这就是一切都在这里的原因”，这真的很有趣。

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:47.079
But I think the sense of payoff that is tied to the longevity and serialized nature of the show is why people keep coming back to the show, like it's some sort of holy shrine because it rewards your fidelity to it.
但我觉得，这种与剧集的长期性和连续剧属性紧密相连的回报感，正是人们不断回看这部剧的原因，就好像它是某种圣地一样，因为它回报了你对它的忠诚。

00:07:47.079 --> 00:07:48.639
Like the more you watch it.
就像你看得越多。

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:50.560
It's like, you know, there's- It is, it's little clues.
这就好比，你知道，有——就是这样，一些小线索。

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.560
I'm gonna, I know what that clue is gonna, I know what the payoff's gonna be to that clue.
我会，我知道那个线索会——我知道那个线索会带来什么样的回报。

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:54.759
And people like that.
人们喜欢这种感觉。

00:07:54.759 --> 00:08:07.920
There's a thing, I think this is from the Bhagavad Gita, forgive me if it's not, but there's some line essentially from like Vedic something where it says, the first taste of ego is sweet, but its fruits are bitter.
有一个说法，我想这是出自《薄伽梵歌》，如果不是的话请原谅我，但基本上是源自吠陀经文里的一句话，上面说，对自我的初次品尝是甜蜜的，但它的果实是苦涩的。

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.279
And the first taste of God is bitter, but its fruits are sweet.
而对上帝的初次品尝是苦涩的，但它的果实是甜蜜的。

00:08:11.279 --> 00:08:17.319
And I feel like what the network was saying was like, give us fruit, sweet, sweet, sweet, give us a thing.
我觉得电视网当时的意思就像是，给我们果实，甜的，甜的，甜的，给我们点东西。

00:08:17.319 --> 00:08:20.800
And then it curdles over time because it's too easy.
然后随着时间推移，它变质了，因为它太容易得到了。

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:28.199
But what you guys were saying was like, we're gonna give you something a little more challenging that will ultimately down the line be more rewarding for you.
但你们当时的意思是，我们要给你们一些更有挑战性的东西，最终在未来会给你们带来更多的回报。

00:08:28.199 --> 00:08:30.519
Yeah, it's kind of the ultimate, wait for it.
对，这有点像是终极的“拭目以待”。

00:08:30.519 --> 00:08:32.759
Wait for it is kind of the mantra of the series.
“拭目以待”可以说是这部剧的座右铭。

00:08:32.759 --> 00:08:33.600
The whole mantra.
整个剧的座右铭。

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.840
That is the guiding aesthetic of the whole series.
那是整个剧集的指导美学。

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.759
And the last line of the season.
也是这一季的最后一台词。

00:08:37.759 --> 00:08:40.159
Yeah, which is my favorite, yeah, we'll get there.
对，这也是我最喜欢的，对，我们会讲到那里的。

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:41.080
We'll get there in the next one.
我们会在下一集里讲到。

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.799
But that is one of my, maybe my favorite ending of the season.
但那是我最喜欢的——也许是我最喜欢的季度结尾。

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:46.720
We haven't had as the ending of this season, but here we are in a second of the last episode.
我们还没把它当作这一季的结尾，但我们现在来到了倒数第二集。

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.419
I just realized my voice is that I just want to say that I've had this upper respiratory infection for the last couple of days.
我刚刚意识到我的声音——我只想说我过去几天得了上呼吸道感染。

00:08:51.419 --> 00:08:52.419
These are our challenges.
这些都是我们的挑战。

00:08:52.419 --> 00:08:59.139
I'm just, I'm getting through it and it hasn't been fun, but I'm well enough to be here today and I'm thrilled to be here today.
我只是，我正在挺过去，这并不好受，但我今天身体足够好来到这里，我很高兴能来到这里。

00:08:59.139 --> 00:09:07.820
I was really, I was really taken, my wife and I watched the last three episodes all last night.
我真的，我真的很震撼，昨晚我和我妻子一口气看了最后三集。

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:16.743
And I had this experience, I want to tell you this, it was during the third, it was during something blue.
我有了这样的体验，我想告诉你，那是在第三集的时候，是在看《Something Blue》的时候。

00:09:16.743 --> 00:09:21.710
And it reminded me of, do you know the actor Victor Garber?
它让我想起了——你认识演员维克托·加博（Victor Garber）吗？

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:23.258
Yeah, a great actor.
认识，一个伟大的演员。

00:09:23.258 --> 00:09:28.909
He played the ship's architect in Titanic and he had a long great career.
他在《泰坦尼克号》里饰演了船舶总设计师，他有着漫长而辉煌的职业生涯。

00:09:28.909 --> 00:09:30.978
Another classic architect like Ted.
又一个像泰德一样的经典建筑师。

00:09:30.978 --> 00:09:32.337
Yeah, exactly, exactly.
对，没错，没错。

00:09:32.337 --> 00:09:37.832
And we did this musical together years ago called She Loves Me and we had a great experience.
我们多年前一起演过一部叫《爱我》（She Loves Me）的音乐剧，我们有过一段很棒的经历。

00:09:37.832 --> 00:09:39.723
So we had a lot of time to chat.
所以我们有很多时间聊天。

00:09:39.723 --> 00:09:44.686
And he was in the original cast of Sweeney Todd, the great Stephen Sondheim musical.
他是伟大的斯蒂芬·桑德海姆（Stephen Sondheim）音乐剧《理发师陶德》（Sweeney Todd）原版卡司的一员。

00:09:44.686 --> 00:09:45.186
Yeah.
对。

00:09:45.186 --> 00:09:47.077
And he played the young Anjanou.
他饰演了年轻的安东尼（Anthony）。

00:09:47.077 --> 00:09:49.027
He sings that great song, Joanna.
他唱了那首著名的歌，《乔安娜》（Joanna）。

00:09:49.027 --> 00:09:57.477
And he said, they opened the show and he got an offer to do, I think this musical called their playing our song, which is a two-hander musical.
他说，演出开演后，他得到了一个出演一部叫《我们在恋爱》（They're Playing Our Song）的音乐剧的机会，这是一部只有两个演员的音乐剧。

00:09:57.477 --> 00:10:01.554
He got an offer to go on the road for a lot of money with a big star.
他得到了一个拿着高薪、跟着一个大明星去外地巡演的机会。

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.703
And he left Sweeney Todd to go do this.
于是他离开了《理发师陶德》去演这个。

00:10:04.703 --> 00:10:06.987
And he thought, this is a good move.
他当时想，这是一步好棋。

00:10:06.987 --> 00:10:08.699
It financially makes sense.
从财务上讲很合算。

00:10:08.699 --> 00:10:13.139
And then he finished the road to where Sweeney Todd was still playing.
然后等他结束巡演回来时，《理发师陶德》还在演。

00:10:13.139 --> 00:10:17.135
He either got asked to go see it or he just decided to drop in.
他要么是应邀去看看，要么就是决定顺便去剧场。

00:10:17.135 --> 00:10:23.161
He's sitting in the audience at Sweeney Todd, this musical that he was in the original cast of.
他坐在《理发师陶德》的观众席里，这部他曾是原版卡司之一的音乐剧。

00:10:23.161 --> 00:10:27.030
And he's sitting there and he goes, oh my god, I was in this?
他坐在那里，然后他说，天哪，我演过这个？

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:30.011
Like he was so stunned by what he was watching.
就好像他被自己正在看的东西惊呆了。

00:10:30.011 --> 00:10:33.183
And he had no sense of it being when he was in it.
当他参演的时候，他根本没有这种感觉。

00:10:33.183 --> 00:10:37.750
When he was in it, how unbelievable it was and how it was breaking form.
当他身处其中时，根本不知道它是多么的不可思议，又是如何打破常规的。

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:39.970
And it was so extraordinary to him.
这一切对他是如此的非凡。

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:42.316
And he couldn't believe he was in it.
他简直不敢相信自己曾参演其中。

00:10:42.316 --> 00:10:48.025
And I had this moment last night watching this where I was like, Oh my God, I was in this.
昨晚我在看这部剧时也有了这种时刻，我当时想，天哪，我演过这个。

00:10:48.025 --> 00:10:50.562
I was in this great, great piece of art.
我曾参与过这样一部伟大、伟大的艺术作品。

00:10:50.562 --> 00:10:51.704
And I didn't know.
而我当时并不知道。

00:10:51.704 --> 00:10:56.207
I mean, I knew like, I would tell you like, Oh my God, that's so funny.
我是说，我知道，就像，我会告诉你，天哪，这太搞笑了。

00:10:56.207 --> 00:10:57.793
Or I think this is great.
或者我觉得这太棒了。

00:10:57.793 --> 00:11:01.979
I knew I was on a clever, wonderful show that was gathering steam.
我知道我参演了一部聪明绝顶、广受好评、渐入佳境的剧集。

00:11:01.979 --> 00:11:02.994
I knew all that.
这些我都懂。

00:11:02.994 --> 00:11:08.830
But I didn't have a sense that I was in some I'm sort of like iconic generationally defined.
但我并没有意识到我身处于某种具有标志性、能定义一代人的作品之中。

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:14.190
That was our hope, but it was so hard for me in the moment to appreciate.
那是我们的期望，但在当时对我来说，要体会到这一点实在是太难了。

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:20.629
And now all these years later, looking back, I really see like, oh my God, I was a part of something so cool.
而现在，多年后的今天，回过头来看，我真的明白，天哪，我曾是如此酷的事物的一部分。

00:11:20.629 --> 00:11:26.029
Totally, it's like the best version of the, it's the positive version of the frog in the boiling water.
完全是这样，这就像是那个“温水煮青蛙”的故事的最佳版本，是积极的版本。

00:11:26.029 --> 00:11:27.029
Right, right.
对，对。

00:11:27.029 --> 00:11:33.909
It's like our boiling water was, we're on a really good show and the frog's boiling water was it's death, but it's the metaphor falls apart at a certain point.
这就好比我们的“温水”是，我们正在拍一部非常棒的剧，而青蛙的“温水”是它的死亡，不过这个比喻在某个时刻就说不通了。

00:11:33.909 --> 00:11:37.909
but it's like you don't, you don't quite know what ecosystem you're in as you're making it.
但这就好比你在制作它的时候，你并不太清楚自己身处于什么样的生态系统中。

00:11:37.909 --> 00:11:38.909
You don't, I'm feeling that too.
你不知道，我也有这种感觉。

00:11:38.909 --> 00:11:41.710
I'm watching it going, this is a good show.
我看着它，心想，这是一部好剧。

00:11:41.710 --> 00:12:01.110
But these final three of season two, and the one we'll talk about next, something blue, the ending of something blue, which we'll get to, I just remember having this feeling editing that episode, shooting that scene of you and Neil, I'm jumping an episode here, sorry, but having this feeling of this is working.
但第二季的最后这三集，以及我们接下来要谈的那集——《Something Blue》，《Something Blue》的结局，我们等会儿会讲到，我只记得在剪辑那一集、拍摄你和尼尔的那场戏时——抱歉，我这里跳过了一集，但当时我有一种感觉：这成功了。

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:05.830
I had a profound sense of wellbeing with this two-part finale, something borrowed and something blue.
对于这两部分的季终集——《Something Borrowed》和《Something Blue》，我产生了一种深深的幸福感。

00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:08.470
I just remember feeling like, we did it.
我只记得当时的感觉是：我们做到了。

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.029
We're on the crab, as they say.
正如他们所说，我们上钩了。

00:12:10.029 --> 00:12:11.029
Yeah.
对。

00:12:11.029 --> 00:12:21.629
And I wrote you this little day to sing to you in New York City.
我在纽约市为你写了这段小曲儿来唱给你听。

00:12:21.629 --> 00:12:25.340
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:29.139
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼（Alex Goldman），播客《Hyper-Fixed》的主持人。

00:12:29.139 --> 00:12:32.659
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会收集听众的问题并尝试为他们解决。

00:12:32.659 --> 00:12:39.659
problems like I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York, or why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
比如“我都30岁了却害怕在纽约开车”，或者“为什么倒车时我不能调节车载音响的音量？”之类的问题。

00:12:39.659 --> 00:12:42.220
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也会解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:48.360
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that caused that problem in the first place.
如果你遇到了问题，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭露导致该问题的隐藏系统。

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.879
That's the Hyper-Fixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的播客《Hyper-Fixed》。

00:12:50.879 --> 00:13:05.970
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at Hyper-FixedPod.com.
你可以在各大播客平台或访问 Hyper-FixedPod.com 找到它。

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:08.059
And now, back to the show.
现在，让我们回到节目中。

00:13:08.059 --> 00:13:15.700
I think that television and I would argue music, they're not opening weekend dependent art forms.
我认为电视——而且我要说音乐也是如此——它们不是那种依赖首映周末表现的艺术形式。

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:17.460
No, it's the long tail.
不，关键在于长尾效应。

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:18.299
It's the long.
这只是漫长的过程。

00:13:18.299 --> 00:13:20.059
It's the initial sales of an album.
这是一张专辑最初的销量。

00:13:20.059 --> 00:13:25.379
Do not speak to how long or how impactful that album will be culturally.
它并不代表这张专辑能持续多久，或者它在文化上有多大的影响力。

00:13:25.379 --> 00:13:41.674
And it's the same thing with TV, especially something we gave people a treasure trove of, I'm actually startled when we, we've covered so much, the first two seasons have so much in them and so many, my memory is, are very strong of, even though I forget large patches.
电视剧也是如此，尤其是我们给观众提供了一整座宝藏。实际上，当回顾我们讲过的内容时，我甚至感到有些惊讶，前两季包含了如此多的内容和细节，我的记忆非常深刻，尽管我也忘了很多碎片。

00:13:41.674 --> 00:13:44.642
I can't believe we have seven more seasons to go.
我真不敢相信我们后面还有七季要拍。

00:13:44.642 --> 00:13:47.428
It's actually, it's how much of this would be.
这实际上是……这会有多长呢。

00:13:47.428 --> 00:13:50.821
I'm curious to see how he's strung it out for that long.
我很想看看他是怎么把剧情拉长这么久的。

00:13:50.821 --> 00:13:52.577
I hope some of them are good.
我希望其中有些部分能保持精彩。

00:13:52.577 --> 00:13:59.180
I was looking at the bar and I was just like, oh my God, we spent so much time now in that bar in that booth.
我看着那个酒吧，心里就在想，天哪，我们现在在这个酒吧的卡座里花了那么多时间。

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:01.239
It's basically a decade or a life.
基本上就是十年，或者说是一生。

00:14:01.239 --> 00:14:03.057
If you round up just a little.
如果稍微凑个整的话。

00:14:03.057 --> 00:14:04.086
Yeah, it's crazy.
是的，太疯狂了。

00:14:04.086 --> 00:14:10.507
But I had such a feeling of like, I remembered the feeling of excitement and spark of the of this episode.
但我有一种强烈的感受，我记得这一集刚开始时的那种兴奋感和灵感。

00:14:10.507 --> 00:14:13.778
And the one who follows like, this is a great episode.
还有接下来的那一集，就像是在说：这是一集很棒的剧集。

00:14:13.778 --> 00:14:16.928
Greg Mayland's did a great job writing this episode.
格雷格·马兰德这一集写得真棒。

00:14:16.928 --> 00:14:28.922
Marshall Millies vows outside when they when the wedding is going so terribly and they relocate outside into one of our one of like four location shoots in the entire history of home at your mother.
当婚礼进行得一团糟，马歇尔和莉莉把誓词搬到户外时，他们搬到了户外，那是《老爸老妈的浪漫史》整部剧历史上仅有的四个外景拍摄地之一。

00:14:28.922 --> 00:14:31.708
And we're at the park right across the street.
我们就位于街对面的公园里。

00:14:31.708 --> 00:14:33.283
It was our big field trip.
那是我们的豪华远足。

00:14:33.283 --> 00:14:35.221
We had the first big field trip.
我们进行了第一次大型外景远足。

00:14:35.221 --> 00:14:41.703
We drove across Pico Boulevard from the Fox lot to a park that is literally right there Rancho Park, right?
我们开车载着皮科大道，从福克斯片场来到了一个离那儿只有几步远的公园——兰乔公园，对吧？

00:14:41.703 --> 00:14:43.157
That's what it's called.
就是那个名字吧？

00:14:43.157 --> 00:14:45.579
It's like Chevy Chevy Hills Rancho Park.
有点像舍维·舍维山兰乔公园。

00:14:46.139 --> 00:14:48.853
And that is that was our huge ambitious location.
那就是我们宏伟壮观的外景地。

00:14:48.853 --> 00:14:53.946
We kept talking about like one day we're going to shoot in New York City and never happened.
我们一直讨论说总有一天要去纽约市拍摄，但从未实现。

00:14:53.946 --> 00:14:55.608
But we did get to Rancho Park.
不过我们确实去了兰乔公园。

00:14:55.608 --> 00:15:02.307
We got to Rancho Park and then in like season seven or eight, we got to universe the universal lot to shoot Subway words.
我们去了兰乔公园，然后在第七季或第八季左右，我们去了环球影城片场拍摄地铁场景。

00:15:02.307 --> 00:15:05.186
And that was that was the farthest away we ever got.
那是我们去过最远的地方。

00:15:05.186 --> 00:15:07.180
We never got outside of Los Angeles.
我们从未离开过洛杉矶。

00:15:07.179 --> 00:15:08.996
But boy, that that park was great.
但是天哪，那个公园真的很棒。

00:15:08.996 --> 00:15:14.232
And that yeah, I was texting with Pam earlier and she said, I said, do you remember shooting that?
而且，我之前跟帕姆发过短信，她说，我问：你还记得拍摄那个场景吗？

00:15:14.232 --> 00:15:17.759
Was that right across the street in that park right by the Fox on?
就在福克斯旁边那个公园的街对面吗？

00:15:17.759 --> 00:15:21.820
She said, yes, I remember, I just remember it was like having class outside.
她说：是的，我记得，我只记得那感觉就像是在室外上课。

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:23.052
That's exactly what it felt like.
感觉确实如此。

00:15:23.052 --> 00:15:24.396
We were all having such a good time.
我们当时都玩得很开心。

00:15:24.396 --> 00:15:25.890
And she said to try to send some photos.
她说她要发一些照片过来。

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:30.820
I don't like if she doesn't put some people, you know how you could to kind of either get a lift down in New York Street or walk it.
我不知道她有没有发一些人，你知道你可以坐电梯去纽约街或者走过去。

00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:31.899
It's right there.
它就在那里。

00:15:31.899 --> 00:15:33.860
I always like walking.
我一直喜欢走路。

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:38.379
It is a literal stone's throw away, but it's a beautiful park.
真的只有一步之遥，但那是个美丽的公园。

00:15:38.379 --> 00:15:39.340
It really did the trick.
它确实起到了很好的效果。

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:41.220
It felt east coast somehow.
不知何故，它感觉很有东海岸的风情。

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:42.100
It felt very green.
感觉非常翠绿。

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:44.179
It felt it did not feel like Southern California.
感觉一点也不像南加州。

00:15:44.179 --> 00:15:45.019
It looked great.
效果看起来很棒。

00:15:45.019 --> 00:15:47.259
And that whole sequence is so sweet.
而且整个桥段非常温馨。

00:15:47.259 --> 00:15:48.100
They're vows.
他们的誓词。

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:53.340
And the fact that they really get married only with this group and almost feels like the whole group is getting married.
事实上，他们只在这一群人的见证下结婚，感觉就像是整个集体在结婚一样。

00:15:53.340 --> 00:16:06.879
